-- THE TIER'S CENTRAL FACT: tuning trades its targets against each other.
-- Three improve and the FORMATION VOLUME FACTOR GETS WORSE, from -0.309 pct
-- to -1.132, and it was the best-matched target before tuning. Total gas-oil
-- ratio, stock tank gravity and formation volume factor all divide by the
-- same stock-tank volume, so no setting of four knobs makes four coupled
-- targets exact. That frontier is physical rather than numerical: running the
-- solver longer lands in the same place. A fit that improves every target at
-- once usually has more freedom than it has data.
-- THE TIER'S SHARPEST LESSON, and the one that cost the most to find: the
-- saturation pressure is computed by a bisection with a 0.05 psia tolerance,
-- so it is QUANTIZED. The shared Levenberg-Marquardt kernel's default
-- finite-difference step of about one part in a million moved it by less than
-- one quantum, the derivative came back ZERO, and the solver concluded that
-- three of the four knobs did nothing. The regression converged, reported
-- success and reduced the residual, because the one knob whose target was not
-- quantized still worked. Nothing errored and nothing warned. The fix was an
-- explicit absolute Jacobian step, which required a backward-compatible
-- per-parameter option on the shared kernel. Any target from a search with a
-- tolerance, any counted quantity, or any rounded value does the same thing.
--
-- Bank QA before seeding (recorded in migrations/docs/rc6-fluid-pentest.md):
