insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-equation-of-state', 1, 'What does a reservoir engineer mainly need from an equation of state?', '["The volume, since every reservoir calculation is ultimately some kind of conversion between reservoir and surface volumes.", "The density of each phase, from which the formation volume factors and the gravities all follow directly.", "The phase behaviour: given a composition, a pressure and a temperature, how the mixture splits and what is in each phase.", "The fugacity coefficients, since those are what a simulator carries between timesteps as its state."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The volumes are a secondary output and the cubic family gets them worst. What makes the family useful is that the mathematics produces the split rather than having it bolted on.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-equation-of-state', 2, 'What do the two van der Waals corrections to the ideal gas law represent?', '["That molecules occupy space, so a covolume is subtracted, and that they attract each other, so a pressure term is subtracted.", "That molecules collide inelastically, and that the container walls exert an attraction of their own.", "That the temperature is not uniform throughout a real gas, and that the molecules themselves carry internal degrees of freedom.", "That the gas is compressible, and that the compressibility itself varies with pressure."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Multiply the resulting expression through and it becomes a cubic in volume, which is where the family name comes from and the source of everything useful about it.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-equation-of-state', 3, 'Why does the cubic FORM matter?', '["Because a cubic can be solved in closed form, which is what makes a compositional simulation fast enough to run.", "Because a cubic has one or three real roots, so the equation itself says whether there is one phase or two.", "Because a cubic is the lowest order that can reproduce the critical point''s inflection in the isotherm.", "Because a cubic guarantees a unique liquid root, which is what the flash calculation needs to converge."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Three roots means the equation is describing a two-phase region: the largest is the vapour volume, the smallest the liquid, and the middle one is physically meaningless. Nothing simpler does that.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-equation-of-state', 4, 'Why are more accurate multi-parameter equations of state not used for reservoir fluids?', '["They are proprietary, so a commercial simulator cannot embed them without licensing each substance.", "They were developed for cryogenic and refrigeration work and have never been fitted to hydrocarbons above C6.", "They require an iterative solve for the volume, which a cubic avoids by having a closed-form root.", "They need per-substance parameters that do not exist for a pseudo-component, they do not extend to mixtures easily, and they are slow."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The thing we most need to model, the heavy fraction, is the thing they cannot describe. A cubic loses on the criterion that matters least, which is pure-substance accuracy.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-equation-of-state', 5, 'A two-parameter cubic buys phase behaviour cheaply. What does it get wrong?', '["Liquid densities, typically several percent out and systematically so, which is why volume translation exists.", "Vapour densities at high pressure, where the repulsion term dominates and the two-parameter form fails.", "The critical point itself, which a two-parameter cubic places at the wrong compressibility for every substance.", "Convergence near the critical region, where the three roots approach each other and the solve becomes ill-conditioned."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The critical compressibility IS misplaced by a cubic and near-critical convergence IS difficult, but neither is the cost the volume translation was invented to fix.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-equation-of-state', 6, 'Which cubic does the engine implement, and is that a claim?', '["Both Peng-Robinson and Soave-Redlich-Kwong, selected by the caller at run time, since neither is better in general.", "Peng-Robinson 1976, with the 1978 modification available as an option for heavy components.", "Peng-Robinson 1978 only, and it is a stated scope decision rather than a claim that Soave-Redlich-Kwong is wrong.", "A generalised two-parameter cubic, with Peng-Robinson and Soave-Redlich-Kwong as coefficient sets."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Peng-Robinson generally does better on liquid densities, which is why it is more common in reservoir work. Restricting scope and saying so is different from asserting the alternative is wrong.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-equation-of-state', 7, 'Where do the constants 0.457235529 and 0.077796074 come from?', '["A least-squares fit to the pure-substance volumetric data available when the equation was published.", "Exact algebra: they follow from requiring the first and second derivatives of pressure with respect to volume to vanish at the critical point.", "A convention adopted to make the equation reduce to van der Waals in the limit of zero acentric factor.", "The critical compressibility factor of methane, which was taken as the reference substance for the family."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is what defines a critical point. They are not fitted constants, which is worth knowing when deciding what in the equation may legitimately be adjusted.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-equation-of-state', 8, 'What is the role of the alpha function?', '["It corrects the covolume for temperature, which is what makes the liquid branch behave at low reduced temperature.", "It interpolates between the vapour and liquid roots, so that the equation is continuous through the critical point.", "It applies the volume translation, which is why the translation cancels in the equilibrium calculation.", "It carries all the temperature dependence of the attraction term, and the acentric factor enters through its kappa."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That is the whole role of the acentric factor in the equation: it decides how fast the attraction weakens with temperature.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-equation-of-state', 9, 'What did the 1978 modification change?', '["It added a cubic branch for kappa above an acentric factor of 0.49, where the original quadratic turns over.", "It replaced the alpha function entirely with a new form that is smoothly continuous through the critical point.", "It introduced the volume translation, which the 1976 formulation had no mechanism for.", "It changed the omega constants to values fitted against a wider set of pure substances."]'::jsonb, 0, 'For light components the two are identical, because they never reach the branch. For the heavy pseudo-component that carries most of a black oil''s mass it is a different correlation for kappa.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-equation-of-state', 10, 'Good Oil''s C7+ has an acentric factor of 0.6690835265426222. What follows?', '["It takes the 1976 branch, since the switch is at 0.7 rather than at 0.49 in the engine''s implementation.", "It falls outside both branches, so the engine extrapolates the cubic form and raises a warning.", "It takes the 1978 branch, which makes the modification structural rather than optional for this fluid.", "It is unusually low for a heavy pseudo-component, which suggests the characterization should be checked."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every heavy pseudo-component in every reservoir fluid lands on that branch. An implementation carrying only the 1976 form is a different equation for the component that matters most, and it does not announce itself.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-equation-of-state', 11, 'Why does the covolume mix linearly and the attraction quadratically?', '["Because the covolume is a first-order property and the attraction a second-order one in the virial expansion.", "Because molecular volumes add, while attraction is between two molecules and therefore runs over every pair.", "Because the linear rule is exact and the quadratic one is an approximation adopted for tractability.", "Because the covolume has no binary interaction parameter, so there are no pairs for it to sum over."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The geometric mean of the two pure attractions is the natural first guess for how strongly an i attracts a j, and the binary interaction parameter corrects that guess.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-equation-of-state', 12, 'What is a binary interaction parameter?', '["A measure of how strongly two components repel, fitted to their mixture''s excess volume.", "A scaling on the covolume of a pair, which corrects for the packing of unlike molecules.", "A dimensionless group formed from the two components'' critical temperatures, computed rather than fitted to anything.", "A fitted correction on the geometric mean of two components'' attractions, zero when the mean is exactly right."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Real pairs deviate, particularly between molecules of very different size or polarity, and it is fitted to binary phase-equilibrium data. Typical hydrocarbon values are a few hundredths.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-equation-of-state', 13, 'How does the engine handle binary interaction parameters for the C7+ pseudo-component?', '["A modified Chueh-Prausnitz correlation for the C1 pair, the table''s heaviest column reused for the inerts, and zero for other hydrocarbon pairs.", "It sets them all to zero, since a pseudo-component has no published pair data of any kind.", "It interpolates the published table against molecular weight, extrapolating beyond its heaviest entry.", "It fits them during the tuning, which is why the pseudo-component''s interactions are lab tuned rather than published."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s documentation records that a different published choice was investigated and could not be verified in any accessible source, so the modified Chueh-Prausnitz was shipped and the substitution written down.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-equation-of-state', 14, 'What is the equilibrium condition, and what follows from it?', '["Equal chemical potential for every component, from which the fugacities follow by exponentiation.", "Equal pressure and equal temperature in both of the phases, from which the two compositions follow by material balance alone.", "Equal fugacity for every component in both phases, from which the K values follow as the ratio of the fugacity coefficients.", "Equal K values across components, which is what the Rachford-Rice equation enforces."]'::jsonb, 2, 'K values are not an independent thing to be found: they follow from the fugacity coefficients, which follow from the equation of state. K above one means the component prefers the vapour.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-equation-of-state', 15, 'Why does volume translation not appear in the equilibrium calculation?', '["Because it is applied only to the reported outputs, after the flash has already converged.", "Because a constant shift changes every component''s fugacity in a phase by the same factor, so the ratio across the phases is unchanged.", "Because it is small enough that its effect falls below the tolerance the outer iteration converges to.", "Because it is applied to the molar volume and the fugacity depends on the compressibility factor instead."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine therefore reports untranslated fugacities and applies the translation only to volumes and densities. That is an unusually clean result: it improves densities and provably leaves the phase behaviour alone.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-flash-and-envelope', 1, 'Why can a flash calculation not decide by itself how many phases there are?', '["Because it solves for the split at a given set of K values and the K values are an input rather than an output.", "Because it will converge on a trivial solution where both phases have the feed composition, which satisfies equal fugacities and means nothing.", "Because it assumes two phases in its material balance, so a single-phase feed makes the equations inconsistent.", "Because its convergence tolerance cannot distinguish a very small vapour fraction from zero."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It can also converge on the trivial answer for a mixture that IS two phase, if the starting estimate is poor. Detecting it afterwards is possible and fragile.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-flash-and-envelope', 2, 'What question does a stability test ask?', '["Whether adding an infinitesimal amount of some trial phase would lower the total Gibbs energy.", "Whether the cubic returns three real roots at these conditions, which is the signature of a two-phase region.", "Whether the K values from Wilson''s estimate straddle one, which indicates a mixture that will split.", "Whether the feed composition lies inside the phase envelope traced at the stated temperature."]'::jsonb, 0, 'If a trial phase of any composition lowers the energy, the mixture is unstable and will split. That is a stronger and more reliable test than counting roots.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-flash-and-envelope', 3, 'Why is the stability test run from both sides?', '["Because the two sides use different starting estimates, and running both improves the convergence rate.", "Because the vapour side detects dew points and the liquid side detects bubble points, and any given fluid may turn out to have either of them.", "Because a mixture can be unstable to a vapour-like trial phase and not to a liquid-like one, and testing one side misses those cases.", "Because the Gibbs energy surface has two minima, and only running both finds the global one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A mixture mostly liquid just below its bubble point is unstable to a vapour-like trial and not to a liquid-like one. Test only the liquid side and you wrongly conclude it is stable.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-flash-and-envelope', 4, 'What does the stability test return besides a yes or no?', '["The vapour fraction at which the second phase first appears, which the flash then refines.", "The Gibbs energy difference, which quantifies how far from stable the mixture is.", "The identity of the component that drives the instability, which seeds the K value update.", "A trial composition that makes a good starting estimate for the flash that follows."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That makes the two calculations a pair rather than a check followed by the real work: the stability test does most of the work of finding where the second phase is.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-flash-and-envelope', 5, 'Where is the stability test hardest?', '["Near the critical point, where the two phases become similar and the energy surface flattens.", "At very low pressure, where the vapour phase is nearly ideal and the trial compositions are unbounded.", "For mixtures with a large inert content, where the binary interaction parameters dominate the mixing.", "For mixtures with a lumped pseudo-component, since its properties are correlated rather than measured."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s phase envelope tracing is built on the stability flag and its documentation records near-critical truncation as a known limit: very narrow two-phase windows can be missed.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-flash-and-envelope', 6, 'What is Wilson''s correlation used for?', '["A first estimate of the saturation pressure, which the logarithmic scan then brackets more precisely.", "A starting estimate for the K values, which does not need to be accurate as long as it puts the light components above one.", "A check on the converged flash result, since a properly converged flash ought to agree with Wilson to within a few percent or so.", "The initial composition of the trial phase in the stability test, which is then held fixed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It is not accurate and it does not need to be. Putting the light components above one and the heavy ones below is enough for an iteration to take over.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-flash-and-envelope', 7, 'What does the Rachford-Rice equation solve for?', '["The K values themselves, given the vapour fraction and the two phase compositions.", "The fugacity coefficients, given the composition and the root of the cubic.", "The vapour fraction, given the K values and the feed composition.", "The saturation pressure, given the temperature and the feed composition."]'::jsonb, 2, 'One equation in one unknown. The K values are an input to it, and updating them from the equation of state is the outer loop the whole flash sits inside.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-flash-and-envelope', 8, 'Why is Rachford-Rice preferred to either sum-to-one constraint on its own?', '["Because subtracting the two gives a function that is strictly monotonic in the vapour fraction, so it has exactly one root.", "Because taking the difference eliminates the K values entirely, leaving behind an equation that can then be solved in closed form.", "Because either constraint alone is satisfied by the trivial solution, which the difference excludes.", "Because the difference is dimensionless, so a single convergence tolerance works across all fluids."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Either constraint on its own gives a function with poles and multiple roots, and a solver on it is unreliable. A strictly monotonic function cannot mislead a root-finder.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-flash-and-envelope', 9, 'How is Rachford-Rice solved in practice?', '["Bisection alone, since the monotonic function guarantees convergence and speed is not critical here.", "A closed-form root, since the equation reduces to a polynomial of degree equal to the component count.", "Successive substitution on the vapour fraction, accelerated by the same method the outer loop uses.", "Newton with a bisection safeguard: take the Newton step if it stays in the bracket, otherwise bisect."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That gives Newton''s speed with bisection''s guarantee. Newton alone can step outside the valid interval.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-flash-and-envelope', 10, 'Why does the engine allow a negative flash?', '["Because a negative vapour fraction is physically meaningful for a retrograde condensate below its dew point.", "Because a vapour fraction outside zero to one says the mixture is single phase and how far into that region it is, and the smoothness helps the outer iteration.", "Because clipping at the boundary would break the material balance the flash is built on.", "Because the stability test can be skipped when the negative flash itself reports single phase."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The physical answer is read off afterwards: negative means all liquid, above one means all vapour. Treating it as a failure loses the information and makes the iteration behave worse at exactly the conditions where it needs to behave well.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-flash-and-envelope', 11, 'What are the four steps of the outer loop?', '["Guess a vapour fraction, compute the compositions, evaluate the cubic roots, and update the vapour fraction.", "Run the stability test, solve Rachford-Rice, apply the volume translation, and check the material balance.", "Guess K values, solve Rachford-Rice for the split, compute fugacity coefficients from the equation of state, update the K values from their ratio.", "Estimate the saturation pressure, bracket it, bisect, and classify the boundary that was found."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That is successive substitution, and the engine accelerates it with the general dominant eigenvalue method, which extrapolates the sequence of updates without changing where it converges to.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-flash-and-envelope', 12, 'How does the engine''s oracle verify the flash?', '["By implementing the same physics with completely different numerics: plain successive substitution and a bisection root-finder, with no acceleration and no negative flash.", "By comparing against published flash results for the same fluids at the same conditions.", "By checking that the fugacities of every component match between the two phases to within a tolerance.", "By re-running the flash from several different starting estimates and confirming they converge together."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two agree to around one part in ten billion across a grid of thirty-nine states, which says the acceleration changes the number of iterations rather than the answer.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-flash-and-envelope', 13, 'How does the engine find a saturation pressure?', '["A Newton solve on the condition that the vapour fraction equals exactly zero, started from Wilson''s estimate.", "A logarithmic scan on pressure until the stability flag changes, then a bisection to 0.05 psia.", "A closed-form expression obtained by setting the vapour fraction to zero in the Rachford-Rice equation.", "A search along the phase envelope, which is traced first and then interpolated at the stated temperature."]'::jsonb, 1, 'There is no closed form: the boundary is where the stability test changes its answer, and finding it means looking. The scan is logarithmic because saturation pressures span orders of magnitude across fluids.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-flash-and-envelope', 14, 'How does the engine tell a bubble point from a dew point?', '["By the sign of the slope of the envelope at that temperature, which is positive for bubble points.", "By whether the temperature is below or above the mixture''s critical temperature, which the characterization gives.", "By the vapour fraction the flash returns at the boundary, which is zero for a bubble point and one for a dew point.", "By probing just inside the two-phase region and asking which phase is the minor one."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It uses an inset ladder at one, three and six percent, with a liquid-likeness fallback near the critical point where the flash probe cannot classify, and it marks which method produced the classification.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-flash-and-envelope', 15, 'What does the retrograde region on a phase envelope mean?', '["That the envelope has two branches at the same temperature, so a fluid can be two phase in two separate windows.", "That the critical point lies to the right of the cricondentherm, which is what distinguishes a condensate.", "That lowering the pressure at constant temperature causes liquid to condense out rather than vaporise.", "That the dew point curve is traced backwards, so the tracing has to run from high temperature to low."]'::jsonb, 2, 'It sits between the critical point and the cricondentherm on the dew point branch, and it is what gives gas condensates their behaviour and their name.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-tuning', 1, 'What is the rule that decides which parameters may be knobs?', '["Tuning adjusts constructed quantities to reproduce measured ones, and never adjusts a measured quantity.", "Tuning adjusts whichever parameters have the largest effect on the residual, since that converges fastest.", "Tuning adjusts parameters that carry no published uncertainty, since those are the ones nobody can defend.", "Tuning adjusts as few parameters as there are targets, so the system is exactly determined."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It is the same rule the simulation course applied to its structural calibration: tune the unconstrained parameter against the constrained quantity, never the reverse.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-tuning', 2, 'Which of these is a constructed quantity?', '["Methane''s critical temperature, which the equation of state consumes in exactly the same way.", "The reported composition, which was recombined rather than measured directly on the wellstream.", "The C7+ acentric factor.", "The C7+ specific gravity, which was measured on a separated fraction rather than on the fluid."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The composition is recombined from measurements and the C7+ gravity is measured on a separated fraction; both are data. The acentric factor of a pseudo-component came out of Lee-Kesler applied to a construct.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-tuning', 3, 'What would happen if a regression were allowed to move methane''s critical temperature?', '["It would not, because the bounds on the library components are set to zero width by the engine.", "It would, because methane at 0.3647 has a large effect on the saturation pressure and a least-squares solver takes efficient routes.", "It would, but the effect would be negligible, since methane''s properties are already close to optimal.", "It would fail to converge at all, because the library component properties are consumed by the characterization step as well as by the mixing rules."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The resulting model would reproduce the four numbers and be wrong about methane everywhere. That is what happens whenever a regression is given a parameter it should not have.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-tuning', 4, 'Why not simply use more knobs?', '["Because each additional knob multiplies the cost of the finite-difference Jacobian by the number of targets.", "Because the bounds interact, so a larger parameter set makes it harder to keep every knob physical.", "Because four is the maximum the Levenberg-Marquardt kernel supports without a rank-deficient Jacobian.", "Because a regression with more freedom than data will use it, absorbing the measurement noise into parameters that then predict badly."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Four targets can be fitted exactly by four well-chosen parameters and by eight in infinitely many ways. In this domain that is not academic: an overfitted model reproduces its study and forecasts the field wrongly.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-tuning', 5, 'What do the bounds on the knobs encode?', '["That the characterization correlations were uncertain by a certain amount, and not by an unlimited amount.", "That the solver must remain inside a region where the Jacobian is well conditioned.", "That the tuned pseudo-component must reproduce the reported molecular weight and specific gravity.", "That the resulting model must stay within the validity ranges of the correlations that produced the starting values."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A critical temperature multiplier that can range over a factor of two is not a knob, it is permission to invent a substance. The bounds are physics rather than numerics.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-tuning', 6, 'The engine reports which bounds were hit. What does a bound hit mean?', '["The knob has reached its optimum, since a constrained optimum commonly lies on the boundary.", "The regression has not converged, and should be restarted from a point closer to the bound.", "The fit wanted to go further than physics allows, which is a signal that something else is wrong rather than a success.", "The bounds were set too tightly for this particular fluid and should be widened until the knob sits comfortably inside them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Usually it means the composition, the characterization method or the target data is wrong. On Good Oil, none of the four is hit.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-tuning', 7, 'What are the four knobs?', '["Multipliers on all four of the C7+ critical temperature, critical pressure, acentric factor and volume shift.", "Multipliers on the C7+ critical temperature and pressure, and absolute values for the C1 to C7+ interaction parameter and the volume shift.", "Absolute values for the C7+ critical temperature and critical pressure, and multipliers on the binary interaction parameter and the volume shift.", "One multiplier per component group: the light ends, the intermediates, the heavy ends and the inerts."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every one acts on the C7+ pseudo-component and nothing else, because those are the constructed quantities and everything else was measured.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-tuning', 8, 'Which knob cannot affect the saturation pressure at all?', '["The critical pressure multiplier, since the saturation pressure depends on the critical temperature instead.", "The binary interaction parameter, since it enters only the attraction and not the phase split.", "None of them; all four affect the saturation pressure through the mixture''s a and b parameters.", "The volume shift, because the translation cancels in the equilibrium."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That gives it an almost private effect on the stock tank gravity, which makes the regression better conditioned than four knobs all fighting over the same residuals.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-tuning', 9, 'Why are two knobs multipliers and two absolute?', '["Because a multiplier keeps a positive quantity positive, and the other two may legitimately be negative.", "Because the criticals enter the equation non-linearly and the other two enter linearly, so the scaling differs.", "Because the form encodes how much the starting value is worth: the Kesler-Lee criticals are meaningful starting points and the other two starting values are guesses.", "Because the engine''s bounds are relative for the criticals and absolute for the other two, and the knob form has to match."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A multiplier of 0.996 says the correlation was nearly right, which is a statement a reader can evaluate. For a barely-constrained interaction parameter the starting value is a guess and the tuned value is the answer.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-tuning', 10, 'The tuned criticals barely move and the volume shift moves by about a fifth. What does that say?', '["That the Kesler-Lee characterization was close and the Jhaveri-Youngren volume shift correlation was not.", "That the regression is under-determined in the criticals, so the prior pull held them near their starting values.", "That the criticals hit their bounds early and the solver spent its remaining freedom on the volume shift.", "That the saturation pressure target was satisfied first, leaving the other three to be fitted by the shift alone."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That is exactly what the Professional tier predicted from the mechanisms, and seeing the regression confirm it independently is worth more than either statement alone. No knob hits a bound.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-tuning', 11, 'What does the prior pull do that the bounds cannot?', '["It prevents the solver from taking steps larger than the bounds allow, which is what keeps it inside them.", "It stops an under-determined problem from wandering arbitrarily inside the bounds, so the model degrades gracefully toward the untuned one.", "It weights the targets against one another, so that a well-measured target ends up counting for rather more than a poorly measured one does.", "It penalises correlated knobs, which is how the regression avoids fitting the same physics twice."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With one target and three knobs affecting it, there is a family of settings that hit it exactly and the solver picks one arbitrarily. The prior makes that choice explicit and states what it prefers.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-tuning', 12, 'What is the prior pull saying?', '["That the untuned model is the best estimate available until the measured targets prove otherwise beyond some threshold.", "That a small adjustment is more likely to be correct than a large one, which is a statement about physics.", "That the characterization correlations are worth something: absent evidence, prefer them, and with evidence, move.", "That the four knobs are equally uncertain, so none should move further than the others without cause."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The starting values came from published methods fitted to real data. They are not measurements of this fluid and they are not nothing, and a weak pull toward them expresses exactly that.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-tuning', 13, 'What is the Jacobian gotcha in this regression?', '["The Jacobian is singular when two knobs affect the same target identically, which the volume shift and the shift multiplier do.", "The finite-difference step is taken in the tuned parameter space rather than the physical space, so the scaling comes out wrong for the two multipliers.", "The Jacobian is computed once at the starting point and reused, which is invalid once the knobs have moved substantially.", "The saturation pressure is quantized to the bisection tolerance, so a small finite-difference step moves it by nothing and returns a zero derivative."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The default nudge is about one part in a million and the bisection tolerance is 0.05 psia. Three of the four knobs affect the saturation pressure, and the fit would have stalled with them untouched.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-tuning', 14, 'Why was the quantized-objective failure hard to notice?', '["Because the regression converged, reported success and reduced the residual, since the one knob whose target was not quantized still worked.", "Because the solver logged a warning that was suppressed by default in the engine''s configuration.", "Because the affected knobs moved slightly, so the output looked like a normal under-determined fit.", "Because the tuned model still reproduced the four targets, so the fit appeared to have succeeded fully."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nothing errored and nothing warned. The only way to notice was to look at how far each knob had moved and see that three had barely moved, and then to ask why.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-tuning', 15, 'What is the general form of the trap?', '["Any objective with a discontinuity will produce a Jacobian that alternates sign between iterations.", "Any target computed by a search with a tolerance, any counted quantity, or any rounded value will return a zero finite-difference derivative for a small enough step.", "Any parameter with a multiplicative form will have a derivative that scales with its own value, which unbalances the Jacobian.", "Any regression with more parameters than targets will leave some derivatives exactly zero by construction."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The symptom is always the same: a parameter the solver decides has no effect. The check is to perturb it by hand and see whether the objective changes.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-tuning-costs', 1, 'The saturation pressure goes from plus 5.9 percent to minus 0.08 percent. Why was it the most improvable target?', '["Because it was the target carrying the largest starting error, so the least-squares objective weighted it the most heavily.", "Because three of the four knobs act on the phase equilibrium, and the saturation pressure is a pure equilibrium quantity.", "Because the volume shift acts on it almost privately, in the way it does on the stock tank gravity.", "Because it is computed by a bisection, so the solver can drive it to any value inside the tolerance."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The volume shift is the one knob that cannot affect it at all. The largest starting error was the stock tank gravity, and a bisection tolerance constrains precision rather than what the model predicts.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-tuning-costs', 2, 'The stock tank gravity goes from minus 8.9 API to minus 1.9. Which knob did that?', '["The volume shift, almost single-handedly, because it moves the liquid density without touching the phase split.", "The critical temperature multiplier, since a warmer pseudo-component holds rather more light material in the tank oil.", "The binary interaction parameter with methane, which decides how much methane leaves the stock tank liquid.", "All four together, since the stock tank gravity depends on both what ends up in the liquid and how dense it is."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It is the largest absolute improvement in the table. The gravity does depend partly on what ends up in the liquid, which is why the decoupling is close rather than complete.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-tuning-costs', 3, 'Which target got worse under tuning?', '["The total gas-oil ratio, from plus 3.4 percent to minus 0.82, since the sign flipped across zero.", "The stock tank gravity, since a 1.9 API residual is still outside the engine''s stated tolerance.", "None of them; all four improved, which is what a factor of twenty three in the residual means.", "The formation volume factor, from minus 0.31 percent to minus 1.13."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It was the best-matched target before tuning, the only one an untuned model got right to within half a percent, and the regression gave it up to improve the other three.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-tuning-costs', 4, 'Why does a least-squares fit trade away a target that starts close?', '["Because relative residuals scale with each target''s own magnitude, so the smaller errors end up systematically down-weighted.", "Because the solver visits the targets in order and the last one absorbs whatever freedom remains.", "Because it minimises the sum, so a target contributing little has almost nothing to lose and the far ones have much to gain.", "Because a target already inside its tolerance is dropped from the objective, freeing the knobs for the others."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That is not a defect in least squares; it is what least squares is. If Bo mattered more, the fix is to weight it more heavily, and the fit would then give up more elsewhere.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-tuning-costs', 5, 'Why can better knobs not avoid the trade?', '["Because the coupling is physical: total gas-oil ratio, stock tank gravity and formation volume factor all divide by the same stock-tank volume.", "Because the four knobs are correlated, so the Jacobian is nearly rank-deficient whatever they are.", "Because the bounds prevent the knobs from reaching the region where all four targets can be satisfied.", "Because the prior pull holds the knobs near their starting values and therefore near the untuned residual."]'::jsonb, 0, 'One knob sets how dense the stock tank liquid is. Making it lighter necessarily changes its volume, and two of the other targets divide by that volume. No arrangement of four knobs escapes it.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-tuning-costs', 6, 'What would escape the coupling?', '["A weighted objective that suppresses the two coupled targets, leaving the third to be fitted alone.", "More independent physics: splitting the plus fraction so the stock tank liquid''s composition can change independently of its density.", "A sequential fit, tuning the equilibrium knobs first and the volume shift afterward on the residual.", "A larger set of separator tests, which would over-determine the stock tank volume and pin it directly."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That brings its own problems, which the Professional tier set out: unmeasurable choices, more parameters than data, and a model nobody can check.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-tuning-costs', 7, 'What should be suspicious about a fit where every target improves?', '["Nothing; a fit that improves every target is simply a well-posed problem correctly solved.", "It suggests the targets were measured on different sub-samples, so together they do not constrain any single model.", "It means the prior pull was set too weakly, letting the knobs move further than the data supports.", "It usually has more parameters than targets, or targets that were nearly independent, or it has not converged."]'::jsonb, 3, 'With four knobs and four coupled targets there is a surface of achievable error combinations, and improving one point on it moves others. The frontier is real.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-tuning-costs', 8, 'The residual falls by a factor of 23.157104602764026. What does that number mean?', '["The model is that many times more accurate, averaged across the four targets it was fitted to.", "The uncertainty in every predicted quantity has fallen by the square root of that factor.", "The sum of squared relative errors is that many times smaller, which is dominated by the two targets that started worst.", "The tuned model reproduces the study to within roughly a twenty-third of the untuned model''s error on each of the targets."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A single aggregate hides that one target degraded, which is why the ledger is per target and the aggregate is a footnote.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-tuning-costs', 9, 'What is left in the residual after tuning?', '["The frontier: where the parameterisation runs out, rather than solver error.", "Measurement noise in the four laboratory targets, which no model can fit and none should try to.", "The contribution of the prior pull, which prevents the knobs from reaching the true minimum.", "Numerical error in the saturation pressure, which is quantized to the bisection tolerance."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Running the solver longer, or from a different start, lands in the same place. Distinguishing a solver that stopped early from a model at its frontier is done by restarting from a different point.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-tuning-costs', 10, 'How does the counting actually work out for four knobs and four targets?', '["Four against four exactly, which is why the fit is nearly exact and the residual is small.", "Three knobs act on the equilibrium and three targets are coupled, so it is closer to three parameters against under four constraints.", "Four knobs against two independent constraints, since the gas-oil ratio and gravity carry the same information.", "It cannot be counted, since the relationship between knobs and targets is non-linear throughout."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The volume shift cannot affect the equilibrium at all, and three targets share the stock-tank volume. Counted properly it is a well-posed least-squares problem rather than a solvable system.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-tuning-costs', 11, 'What is the parallel with the simulation course''s calibration?', '["Both calibrations tuned a parameter that no measurement constrained, which is the shared rule.", "Both of them reported a residual that turned out to be the size of the smallest step the model could actually take.", "Both used a bisection, which is why both are limited by the tolerance the search converged to.", "One degree of freedom fixes one number: it could match the booked volume or the booked area, not both."]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is the same statement with larger numbers. You can match as many independent quantities as you have independent parameters, and both words are doing work.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-tuning-costs', 12, 'What five lines does a tuned model''s write-up need?', '["The knobs, the bounds, the solver, the iteration count and the final residual.", "The composition, the characterization, the flash conditions, the separator train and the targets.", "What was tuned, against what, the ledger, what was held fixed, and what it cost.", "The model, the fluid, the date, the engine version and the person who ran it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Without the scope a reader assumes the whole model was fitted. Without the targets they cannot tell what it was fitted to. Without the cost they will find it themselves.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-tuning-costs', 13, 'Which word should not appear in the write-up?', '["Validated, because the model reproduces these four numbers by construction, which is a calibration and cannot fail.", "Tuned, because it implies an arbitrary adjustment rather than a physically bounded regression against real measurements.", "Matched, because the residual is not zero and the word implies an exact reproduction of the data.", "Fitted, because it suggests a statistical procedure where this was a physically bounded optimisation."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A validation needs something the model was not tuned to. Holding the formation volume factor OUT of the objective and checking it afterward would have been one, and it is worth considering for exactly that reason.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-tuning-costs', 14, 'What is the real risk of an undocumented tuning?', '["The parameters cannot be reproduced, so the model cannot be rebuilt if the file is lost.", "A tuned critical temperature is a fitted parameter wearing the name of a measured quantity, and a later reader will treat it as characterization.", "The tuning may be applied twice by a later user, compounding the adjustment without anybody noticing.", "The engine''s gates will fail on the next release, since the tuned values are not part of the pinned fixture."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is why the report goes with the model as well as in the study document. A tuned model whose tuning lives in somebody''s notebook becomes an untraceable set of parameters within a year.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-what-tuning-costs', 15, 'What is the standard response to the knobs being under-determined?', '["A tighter prior pull, which holds the under-determined knobs at their starting values and thereby removes the ambiguity.", "Fewer knobs, chosen so that the remaining set is exactly determined by the available targets.", "A range: tune against perturbed targets or with different weights, and report the spread in the predictions that matter.", "A covariance matrix from the converged Jacobian, which quantifies the uncertainty in each knob directly."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That converts a hidden dependence into a stated sensitivity, which is what a reader can actually use. A covariance estimate is legitimate and it is on the engine''s out-of-scope register.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-tiers-this-course-will-not-grade', 1, 'Which tier does the course refuse to grade?', '["Published method, since a method applied correctly with no independent check cannot support a graded field.", "Lab tuned, since a tuned value depends on the measurements it was tuned to and does not transfer.", "Screening: an estimate nobody has checked against anything, useful for ranking and not for booking.", "Oracle gated, since agreement between two implementations says nothing about whether the physics suits the fluid."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The other three are all graded somewhere in the course. Oracle gated agreement genuinely says nothing about the physics, and that is a limitation of the tier rather than a reason to refuse it.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-tiers-this-course-will-not-grade', 2, 'Why are teaching a number and grading it different acts?', '["Because grading says producing it correctly is a competence worth certifying, which implies the number is worth producing.", "Because teaching requires only an explanation while grading requires a tolerance, which a screening value cannot properly support.", "Because a graded field must be reproducible to sixteen figures and a screening quantity varies between runs.", "Because the capstone chassis cannot express a tolerance wide enough for a factor-of-two uncertainty."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A screening quantity is perfectly reproducible run to run, and a wide tolerance is expressible. What is false is the implication that the number is worth producing to a standard.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-tiers-this-course-will-not-grade', 3, 'Why teach the screening quantities at all?', '["Because the capstone''s other fields depend on them indirectly, so a learner must be able to compute them.", "Because they appear on screens and in reports, and a practitioner who cannot recognise one will use it as though it were measured.", "Because the engine computes them by default, so a user cannot avoid seeing them and must know the mechanism.", "Because they are the quantities most likely to be improved in some future engine release, so the ground has to be prepared for that."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nobody is harmed by not knowing what an LBC viscosity is. People are harmed by seeing one in a table beside a saturation pressure good to a tenth of a percent, formatted identically.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-tiers-this-course-will-not-grade', 4, 'What is the precedent for an ungraded module?', '["The waterflood course''s enhanced recovery screening, which was removed from the capstone after an audit.", "The SCAL course''s averaging module, whose drift was taught but whose graded field was replaced.", "The material balance course''s aquifer module, which grades only the analytical models and not the numerical ones.", "The simulation course''s results-literacy module, which graded nothing because the engine has no flow solver."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The reasoning differs in its detail and is identical in shape: grade what can be checked, teach what a practitioner needs, and say which is which.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-tiers-this-course-will-not-grade', 5, 'What is the LBC correlation?', '["A prediction of hydrocarbon mixture viscosity from composition and density, standard in essentially every commercial simulator.", "A correlation for the critical volume of a pseudo-component, from which all of the transport properties are then said to follow.", "A dilute-gas viscosity model, extended to liquids by an empirical density correction fitted to crude oils.", "A mixing rule for viscosity, applied to component viscosities that are themselves obtained from measurements."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It is worth understanding regardless of what one thinks of it, precisely because it is what everybody uses. It does begin from dilute-gas viscosities and the density correction is the problem.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-tiers-this-course-will-not-grade', 6, 'Why is LBC viscosity a screening quantity?', '["Because the correlation was fitted to gas mixtures and is being extrapolated to liquids without justification.", "Because it depends on the phase densities, which the volume translation has already adjusted by an uncertain amount.", "Because the reduced density needs a critical volume, which for the pseudo-component is the least reliable output of the characterization, and it then enters a fourth power.", "Because no published implementation agrees with any other, so the arithmetic itself is in question."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s tier note gives the numbers: order ten percent on gas, up to a factor of two on oil. The error amplification is severe and it is worst for liquids, where the reduced density is highest.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-tiers-this-course-will-not-grade', 7, 'What is gated about the LBC implementation and what is not?', '["The answer is gated against the study''s reported viscosities, and the implementation is checked only by review.", "The arithmetic is verified by transcription and NIST dilute-gas anchors; the answer is not, because there are no measured viscosities on this fluid to check it against.", "Both are gated, the implementation by transcription and the answer against the eight Coats and Smart fluids.", "Neither is gated, which is what the screening label means and why the module grades nothing."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That distinction is the whole tier system in one example: an implementation can be provably correct and still produce a number nobody should book.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-tiers-this-course-will-not-grade', 8, 'For viscosity on a black oil, which is the better bet?', '["The LBC calculation, since it uses the whole composition and therefore carries more information than a correlation does.", "Either, since both are correlations and the difference in tier reflects documentation rather than accuracy.", "Neither, since viscosity on a black oil should always be measured rather than predicted.", "The Associate tier''s Beal, Beggs-Robinson and Vasquez-Beggs chain, which is published method rather than screening."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That is worth stating plainly. The compositional model''s advantage lies elsewhere, and rigour in one part of a model does not transfer to another part in the same file.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-tiers-this-course-will-not-grade', 9, 'Three features of the Weinaug-Katz calculation amplify uncertainty. What are they?', '["Because the parachor of the pseudo-component is correlated for a substance that does not exist, it enters a difference of two similar quantities, and the result is raised to the fourth power.", "Because Weinaug-Katz was fitted to pure-component surface tensions and mixtures depart from it systematically.", "Because interfacial tension is only meaningful near miscibility, where the model''s phase behaviour is least reliable.", "Because it requires the phase densities, which the LBC viscosity has already shown to be unreliable."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each of those three amplifies uncertainty and together they make interfacial tension the least constrained output of the model.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-tiers-this-course-will-not-grade', 10, 'The engine''s parachor correlation leads with minus 11.4 rather than plus 11.4. Why is that recorded?', '["Because the sign convention differs between the original publication and the reproduction the engine used.", "Because the negative form applies to pseudo-components and the positive form to library components.", "Because a sign error in a leading coefficient produces plausible-looking numbers and survives review, so the check against the source is worth its hour.", "Because the correlation was re-derived by the engine''s authors and the sign follows from their derivation."]'::jsonb, 2, 'It was verified at build time against the source rather than taken from memory. Checking correlation constants against the actual publication, at the time of writing the code, is the practice this records.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-tiers-this-course-will-not-grade', 11, 'What survives the uncertainty in an interfacial tension?', '["The absolute value near miscibility, since the tension goes to zero there whatever the parachors themselves happen to be.", "The trend, the comparison between two gas compositions against the same oil, and a screening estimate of miscibility.", "The ratio between the liquid and vapour contributions, since the parachor errors cancel in the ratio.", "Nothing at all, which is why the module grades none of it and the engine labels it screening."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tension falling as pressure rises toward miscibility is a real and useful signal even when the absolute value is uncertain, and the pseudo-component error is common to both sides of a comparison.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-tiers-this-course-will-not-grade', 12, 'What is the black-oil separator''s gas partition?', '["A simplified flash using two pseudo-components, which is why it agrees with the compositional train on the total.", "An empirical split fitted to separator tests across a range of oils, which is what makes it a published method.", "A material balance on the gas, distributed between stages in proportion to the pressure drop across each.", "A staged-liberation approximation, not a flash, since a black-oil description has no composition to flash."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It telescopes correctly, so the stage volumes sum to the bubble point solution gas ratio by construction. What it does not compute from physics is the split.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-tiers-this-course-will-not-grade', 13, 'When does the black-oil separator stop being enough?', '["When the fluid is near its critical point, where a two-pseudo-component description fails entirely.", "When more than two stages are used, since the approximation was derived for a single separator followed by a tank.", "When the split matters: compressor sizing, stage gas gravities, or optimising a separator train across pressures.", "When the total gas-oil ratio is the answer, since the approximation is least reliable in the aggregate."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The total is exactly what it gets right, by construction. The compositional separator train is the rigorous counterpart and its cost is a composition, which most black-oil users do not have.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-tiers-this-course-will-not-grade', 14, 'What check is available when both a black-oil description and a composition exist?', '["Compare the two separator trains'' total gas-oil ratios, since both telescope to the same total.", "Compare the two saturation pressures, since the black-oil bubble point and the compositional one describe the same boundary.", "Compare the two stock tank gravities, since both are referenced to the same standard conditions.", "Compare the two viscosity chains, since the black-oil and LBC routes are independent of one another."]'::jsonb, 0, 'If they do not agree, the black-oil description and the composition are describing different fluids, which is worth knowing before either is used. It is a free consistency check on two independent descriptions.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-the-tiers-this-course-will-not-grade', 15, 'How should a screening number be reported?', '["To full precision with a footnote, so that a later reader can reproduce the calculation exactly.", "Rounded to one or two figures, with the method named and the status said in words rather than implied by formatting.", "As a range across the plausible inputs, since a single value implies a determinacy the method simply does not possess.", "Only in an appendix, so that it cannot be lifted into a summary and quoted as though it were measured."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Quoting a screening number to sixteen digits is a claim about it whether or not one is intended. Inconsistent precision within a table is a feature when the underlying confidence is inconsistent.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 1, 'Capstone field 1 asks for the tuned saturation pressure. What are the two likely mistakes?', '["Running the search at the separator temperature of 75 F rather than at the reservoir temperature of 220 F.", "Reporting the untuned 2791.10 psia, which the Professional tier grades, or the measured 2634.65, which the fit was aiming at.", "Reporting the pressure at which the tuned flash first returns a non-zero vapour fraction, which only brackets the answer from below.", "Applying the tuning to the library components as well as the pseudo, which shifts the answer substantially."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The check is that it is close to the measurement but not equal to it, and slightly BELOW rather than above, because the tuned error is negative.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 2, 'Capstone field 2 asks for the tuned total gas-oil ratio. What is the check?', '["It sits BELOW the measured 768 scf/stb, where the untuned value sat above it.", "It sits above the measured 768, since tuning raises the gas liberation to match the saturation pressure.", "It equals the separator gas plus the tank gas, which is the identity every train satisfies.", "It falls between the four separator tests the report contains, which bracket the tuned value."]'::jsonb, 0, 'If your answer is above 768 you have the untuned one. Omitting the stock tank stage is the other mistake and it pushes the answer well below the truth for a different reason.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 3, 'Capstone field 3 asks for the tuned stock tank gravity. Where does it sit?', '["At the measured 40.7, since the volume shift knob can reach that target almost independently of the others.", "Below the untuned 31.81, since correcting the saturation pressure makes the stock tank liquid heavier still.", "Between the untuned 31.81 and the measured 40.7, nearer the measurement and about two API light.", "Above the measured 40.7, since the volume shift over-corrects once the other three knobs have all moved."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The volume shift''s effect on the gravity is close to private and it is not complete, because the gravity also depends on WHAT ends up in the liquid, which is equilibrium.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 4, 'Capstone field 4 asks for the tuned volume shift knob. What is the likely mistake?', '["Reporting the shift for a library component rather than for the pseudo, since both appear in the mixture.", "Reporting the shift as a multiplier on the characterized value rather than as the absolute value it is.", "Reporting the shift at reservoir conditions, since the translation is evaluated at each state separately.", "Reporting the untuned characterized shift of 0.15389683656773767, which is where the regression started."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The check is that the tuned value is SMALLER than the characterized starting value, because the untuned model''s oil was too dense and the correction makes it lighter.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 5, 'Capstone field 5 asks for the tuned C1 to C7+ interaction parameter. How do you know it is not one of the multipliers?', '["Because the multipliers are near one and this is a few hundredths, which is the ordinary scale for a hydrocarbon binary interaction parameter.", "Because the multipliers are dimensionless and this carries units of reciprocal temperature.", "Because the multipliers are reported to three figures and the interaction parameter to six.", "Because the multipliers are bounded above one and this knob is bounded below it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'All four are dimensionless, and the bounds on the multipliers straddle one rather than sitting above it. Scale is the distinguishing feature.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 6, 'Capstone field 6 asks for the residual reduction. What form does it take?', '["A percentage, being the fractional reduction in the objective between the untuned and tuned states.", "A difference, being the untuned residual less the tuned one, in the objective''s own units.", "A dimensionless ratio above one, being the sum of squared residuals before the fit over the sum after it.", "A per-target average, being the mean of the four individual improvements weighted by their own tolerances."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Reporting the difference gives a very small number that is obviously not a reduction factor, and reporting one target''s improvement mixes a per-target percentage with an aggregate.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 7, 'What do all six Expert capstone fields have in common?', '["Each of them depends on the volume shift, which is the knob that moves the furthest and therefore comes to dominate every one of the fields.", "Each asks for a value AFTER the regression, and each likely mistake is the corresponding value before it or the target it was aiming at.", "Each can be read from the tuning explorer panel''s ledger without any computation outside the panel.", "Each has a counterpart at the Professional tier, so the pair is the comparison being tested."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Three numbers exist for most of these quantities and only one is the answer. Before submitting, write down which of untuned, measured or tuned you have.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 8, 'Why is the 1978 modification structural rather than optional for this fluid?', '["Because the 1976 quadratic gives a negative kappa at that acentric factor, which makes the alpha function invalid.", "Because the engine implements only the 1978 form, so the question of which branch applies does not arise.", "Because the volume translation was introduced in 1978 and the equation is unusable without it.", "Because the C7+ acentric factor of 0.6690835265426222 is above 0.49, so it takes the cubic kappa branch."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every heavy pseudo-component in every reservoir fluid lands on that branch, and an implementation carrying only the 1976 form is a different equation for the component that matters most.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 9, 'What did the tuned criticals and the tuned volume shift each say about the characterization?', '["That both were close, since no knob hit a bound and the prior pull held them all near their starting values.", "That both were wrong, since a factor of twenty three in the residual implies large corrections throughout.", "That the Kesler-Lee criticals were close and the Jhaveri-Youngren volume shift correlation was not.", "That the criticals were wrong and the shift was close, which is why the equilibrium targets improved most."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The multipliers barely move and the shift moves by about a fifth. That is exactly what the Professional tier predicted from the mechanisms, confirmed independently by the regression.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 10, 'What was the Jacobian gotcha, and why did it matter?', '["The saturation pressure is quantized to 0.05 psia, so a one-part-in-a-million step returned a zero derivative and three of the four knobs were left almost untouched.", "The Jacobian was evaluated once at the starting point, so the later iterations used stale derivatives.", "The multipliers and the absolute knobs have different natural scales, so the Jacobian was badly conditioned.", "The prior pull was included in the residual vector but not in the Jacobian, so the two disagreed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The regression converged, reported success and reduced the residual, because the one knob whose target was not quantized still worked. Nothing errored and nothing warned.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 11, 'What is the general form of the quantized-objective trap?', '["Any objective evaluated by an iterative solver inherits that solver''s convergence noise as derivative noise.", "Any target from a search with a tolerance, any counted quantity, or any rounded value returns a zero finite-difference derivative for a small enough step.", "Any parameter whose effect is smaller than the objective''s own tolerance is unidentifiable from the data.", "Any regression on a non-smooth objective will converge to a point on a discontinuity rather than a minimum."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The symptom is always the same: a parameter the solver decides has no effect. The check is to perturb it by hand and see whether the objective changes.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 12, 'Which target did the joint fit give up, and why could it not be avoided?', '["The stock tank gravity, because the volume shift is bounded and could not reach the measured value.", "The total gas-oil ratio, because the stock tank stage releases gas the model cannot partition correctly.", "The formation volume factor, because total gas-oil ratio, stock tank gravity and formation volume factor all divide by the same stock-tank volume.", "None was given up; all four improved, which is what a twenty-three-fold residual reduction means."]'::jsonb, 2, 'It was the best-matched target before tuning. One knob sets how dense the stock tank liquid is, and making it lighter necessarily changes its volume, which two other targets divide by.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 13, 'What word should not appear in a tuned model''s write-up?', '["Calibrated, because the tuning was against measurements rather than against another model''s output.", "Fitted, because it suggests a statistical procedure where this was a bounded physical optimisation.", "Matched, because the residual is not zero and the word implies exact reproduction.", "Validated, because the model reproduces its four targets by construction."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A validation needs something the model was not tuned to. Holding the formation volume factor out of the objective and checking it afterward would have been one, and it is worth considering for that reason.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 14, 'Which module of this tier grades nothing?', '["The one on screening quantities: LBC viscosity, interfacial tension and the black-oil separator''s gas partition.", "The one on flash and envelope, since the engine''s near-critical tracing has a documented truncation limit.", "The one on the equation of state, since its content is pure theory rather than a computation a learner actually performs.", "None of them; all six modules contribute at least one field to the capstone."]'::jsonb, 0, 'All three are taught with their mechanisms and their limits, and no capstone field touches them. Teaching a number and certifying somebody can produce it are different acts.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 15, 'What did the three tiers of this course have in common?', '["Each tier tuned the model a little further, so the description improved monotonically across the three.", "Nobody measured anything in any of them; what changed is how much was known about where each number came from.", "Each tier worked on a different fluid, so the correlations were tested across a range of oil types.", "Each tier graded a different kind of quantity, moving from volumes through transport properties to compositions."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Associate tier ran correlations on a designed fluid, the Professional tier measured an untuned model against a real study, and this tier opened the model and tuned it.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'Why does the cubic FORM matter for phase behaviour?', '["Because a cubic can be solved in closed form, which is what makes a compositional simulation fast enough to run at all.", "Because a cubic has one or three real roots, so the equation itself says whether there is one phase or two.", "Because a cubic is the lowest order reproducing the critical point''s inflection in the isotherm.", "Because a cubic guarantees a unique liquid root, which is what a flash needs to converge."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Three roots means a two-phase region: largest is the vapour volume, smallest the liquid, middle one meaningless. The mathematics produces the split rather than having it bolted on.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'Why are multi-parameter reference equations not used for reservoir fluids?', '["They need per-substance parameters a pseudo-component does not have, they do not extend to mixtures easily, and they are slow.", "They are proprietary, so a simulator cannot embed them without licensing every substance.", "They were fitted for cryogenic work and never extended above C6 in the hydrocarbon series.", "They require an iterative volume solve, which a cubic avoids with its closed-form root."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The thing we most need to model, the heavy fraction, is what they cannot describe. A cubic loses only on pure-substance accuracy, which matters least here.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'What does a two-parameter cubic get wrong?', '["Vapour densities at high pressure, where the repulsion term dominates and the form fails.", "The critical point, which it places at the wrong compressibility for every substance.", "Convergence near the critical region, where the three roots approach one another.", "Liquid densities, typically several percent and systematically."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The critical compressibility IS misplaced and near-critical convergence IS hard, but the liquid density is the error the volume translation exists to fix.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'Where do 0.457235529 and 0.077796074 come from?', '["A least-squares fit to whatever pure-substance volumetric data happened to be available when Peng and Robinson published.", "The critical compressibility of methane, taken as the family''s reference substance.", "Exact algebra at the critical point, where the first and second derivatives of pressure with respect to volume vanish.", "A convention chosen so the equation reduces to van der Waals at zero acentric factor."]'::jsonb, 2, 'They are not fitted constants, which matters when deciding what in the equation may legitimately be adjusted.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'What did the 1978 modification change, and for whom?', '["It added a cubic kappa branch above an acentric factor of 0.49, which every heavy pseudo-component reaches.", "It replaced the alpha function entirely with a form continuous through the critical point, for near-critical fluids.", "It introduced volume translation, which the 1976 formulation had no mechanism for.", "It refitted the omega constants against a wider set of pure substances, for all components."]'::jsonb, 0, 'For light components the two forms are identical. Good Oil''s C7+ at 0.6690835265426222 takes the 1978 branch, so an implementation carrying only 1976 is a different equation for the component that matters most.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'One mixing rule is a sum and the other a double sum. Why the difference?', '["Because the covolume is first order and the attraction second order in the virial expansion.", "Because molecular volumes add, while attraction is between two molecules and runs over every pair.", "Because the linear rule is exact and the quadratic one is an approximation adopted purely for tractability.", "Because the covolume carries no interaction parameter, so it has no pairs to sum over."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The geometric mean of two pure attractions is the natural first guess for how strongly an i attracts a j, and the binary interaction parameter corrects it.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'Why does the volume translation not appear in the equilibrium?', '["Because it is applied only to reported outputs, after the flash has already converged.", "Because its effect falls below the tolerance the outer iteration converges to.", "Because a constant shift changes every component''s fugacity in a phase by the same factor, leaving the ratio across the phases unchanged.", "Because the fugacity depends on the compressibility factor rather than on the molar volume."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine reports untranslated fugacities and translates only volumes and densities. It improves densities and provably leaves the phase behaviour alone.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'Why can a flash not decide by itself how many phases there are?', '["Because it converges on a trivial solution where both phases carry the feed composition, which satisfies equal fugacities and means nothing.", "Because the K values are an input to it rather than an output, so the phase count is assumed.", "Because its material balance assumes two phases, making a single-phase feed inconsistent.", "Because its tolerance cannot distinguish a very small vapour fraction from exactly zero."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It can converge on the trivial answer for a mixture that IS two phase, if the starting estimate is poor. Detecting it afterwards is possible and fragile.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'What does a stability test ask?', '["Whether the cubic returns three real roots, which signals a two-phase region.", "Whether Wilson''s K values straddle one, which indicates a mixture that will split.", "Whether the feed lies inside the phase envelope traced at that temperature.", "Whether adding an infinitesimal trial phase would lower the total Gibbs energy."]'::jsonb, 3, 'If a trial phase of any composition lowers the energy the mixture is unstable. That is stronger and more reliable than counting roots.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'Why run the stability test from both sides?', '["Because the two sides use different starting estimates and running both improves convergence.", "Because a mixture can be unstable to a vapour-like trial and not to a liquid-like one, and one side alone misses those cases.", "Because the vapour side finds dew points and the liquid side finds bubble points, and any given fluid may turn out to have either.", "Because the energy surface has two minima and only both probes find the global one."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A mixture mostly liquid just below its bubble point is unstable to a vapour-like trial and not a liquid-like one. Test only the liquid side and you wrongly call it stable.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'Why is Rachford-Rice preferred to either sum-to-one constraint?', '["Because taking the difference eliminates the K values entirely, giving an equation that is solvable in closed form.", "Because either constraint alone admits the trivial solution, which the difference excludes.", "Because subtracting them gives a strictly monotonic function of the vapour fraction, with exactly one root.", "Because the difference is dimensionless, so one tolerance works across every fluid."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Either alone gives a function with poles and multiple roots, and a solver on it is unreliable. A strictly monotonic function cannot mislead a root-finder.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'A flash returns a vapour fraction of minus 0.3. Why is that useful rather than an error?', '["Because an excursion outside zero to one says the mixture is single phase and how far, and the smoothness helps the outer iteration.", "Because a negative vapour fraction is physically meaningful for a retrograde condensate.", "Because clipping at the boundary would break the flash''s material balance.", "Because the stability test can then be skipped when the flash itself reports single phase."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The physical answer is read off afterwards: negative means all liquid, above one all vapour. Treating it as failure loses information and makes the iteration behave worse where it needs to behave well.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'There is no closed form for a saturation pressure. How is one obtained?', '["A Newton solve on the condition that the vapour fraction is exactly zero, started from Wilson''s estimate.", "A logarithmic pressure scan until the stability flag changes, then a bisection to 0.05 psia.", "A closed form obtained by setting the vapour fraction to zero in Rachford-Rice.", "Interpolation along a phase envelope traced first across the temperature range."]'::jsonb, 1, 'There is no closed form: the boundary is where the stability test changes its answer. The scan is logarithmic because saturation pressures span orders of magnitude across fluids.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'How does the engine classify a boundary as bubble or dew?', '["By the sign of the envelope''s slope, which is positive on the bubble point branch.", "By whether the temperature is below the mixture''s critical temperature.", "By the vapour fraction at the boundary, zero for a bubble point and one for a dew point.", "By probing just inside the two-phase region and asking which phase is the minor one."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It uses an inset ladder at one, three and six percent, with a liquid-likeness fallback near the critical point, and it marks which method produced the classification.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'What rule decides which parameters may be knobs?', '["Tune whichever parameters most reduce the residual, since that converges fastest.", "Tune exactly as many parameters as there are targets, so the system is determined.", "Tune constructed quantities to reproduce measured ones, and never adjust a measured quantity.", "Tune whichever parameters carry no published uncertainty, since nobody is in a position to defend those."]'::jsonb, 2, 'It is the same rule the simulation course applied to its structural calibration: calibrate the unconstrained parameter against the constrained quantity, never the reverse.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'What would happen if the regression could move methane''s critical temperature?', '["It would, because methane at 0.3647 strongly affects the saturation pressure and a least-squares solver takes efficient routes.", "It would not, since the bounds on library components are set to zero width.", "It would, but negligibly, since methane''s published properties are already near optimal.", "It would fail to converge, since the library properties feed the characterization too."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The model would reproduce the four numbers and be wrong about methane everywhere. That is what happens when a regression is given a parameter it should not have.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'Name the four tuning parameters and say which quantity each acts on.', '["Multipliers on all four of critical temperature, critical pressure, acentric factor and volume shift.", "Multipliers on the C7+ critical temperature and pressure, and absolute values for the C1 to C7+ interaction parameter and the volume shift.", "Absolute values for both criticals, and multipliers on the interaction parameter and the shift.", "One multiplier per component group: light ends, intermediates, heavy ends and inerts."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every one acts on the C7+ pseudo-component and nothing else, because those are the constructed quantities.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'Which knob cannot affect the saturation pressure?', '["The critical pressure multiplier, since the saturation pressure depends on temperature instead.", "The interaction parameter, since it enters the attraction and not the phase split.", "None; all four affect it through the mixture''s a and b parameters.", "The volume shift, because the translation cancels in the equilibrium."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That gives it an almost private effect on the stock tank gravity, which makes the regression better conditioned than four knobs fighting over the same residuals.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'What do the bounds encode, and what does the prior pull add?', '["The bounds keep the Jacobian well conditioned; the prior keeps the solver inside them.", "The bounds are numerical and the prior physical, which is why only the prior is reported.", "The bounds say the characterization was uncertain by a certain amount; the prior stops an under-determined problem wandering arbitrarily inside them.", "The bounds weight the targets; the prior weights the knobs against one another."]'::jsonb, 2, 'With one target and three knobs affecting it there is a family of settings hitting it exactly, and the solver picks one arbitrarily. The prior makes that choice explicit.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'What is the Jacobian gotcha?', '["The saturation pressure is quantized to the bisection tolerance, so a small finite-difference step moves it by nothing and returns a zero derivative.", "The Jacobian is singular because two knobs affect the same target identically.", "The step is taken in tuned rather than physical space, so the multipliers are mis-scaled.", "The Jacobian is computed once and reused after the knobs have moved substantially."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The default nudge is about one part in a million against a 0.05 psia tolerance. Three of the four knobs affect the saturation pressure and the fit would have stalled with them untouched.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'Why was that failure hard to notice?', '["Because the warning it raised was suppressed by default in the engine''s configuration.", "Because the regression converged, reported success and reduced the residual, since the one knob whose target was not quantized still worked.", "Because the affected knobs moved slightly, so it looked like a normal under-determined fit.", "Because the tuned model still reproduced all four targets, so the fit appeared to succeed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nothing errored and nothing warned. The only way to notice was to look at how far each knob moved, see three had barely moved, and ask why.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'One of the four laboratory targets is worse after the fit than before it. Which?', '["The total gas-oil ratio, since its sign flipped from plus 3.4 percent to minus 0.82.", "The stock tank gravity, since 1.9 API is still outside the engine''s stated tolerance.", "None; all four improved, which is what a twenty-three-fold residual reduction means.", "The formation volume factor, from minus 0.31 percent to minus 1.13."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It was the best-matched target before tuning, the only one an untuned model got right to within half a percent, and the regression gave it up to improve the other three.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'A target already within half a percent gets sacrificed by the regression. Why?', '["Because it minimises the sum, so a target contributing little has almost nothing to lose and the far ones much to gain.", "Because relative residuals scale with each target''s own magnitude, so the smaller errors end up systematically down-weighted.", "Because the solver visits targets in order and the last absorbs whatever freedom is left.", "Because a target inside its tolerance is dropped from the objective entirely."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That is not a defect in least squares; it is what least squares is. If Bo mattered more, weight it more heavily and the fit gives up more elsewhere.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'Could a different choice of four knobs have kept every target improving?', '["Because the four knobs are correlated, leaving the Jacobian nearly rank-deficient.", "Because the bounds exclude the region where all four targets can be met at once.", "Because total gas-oil ratio, stock tank gravity and formation volume factor all divide by the same stock-tank volume.", "Because the prior pull holds all four of the knobs near their starting values and therefore near the untuned residual."]'::jsonb, 2, 'One knob sets how dense the stock tank liquid is, and making it lighter necessarily changes its volume. No arrangement of four knobs escapes it.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'A colleague reports a four-target fit in which nothing got worse. What would you ask?', '["Nothing; it simply indicates a well-posed problem that has been correctly solved.", "It usually has more parameters than targets, or nearly independent targets, or it has not converged.", "It suggests the targets came from different sub-samples and constrain no single model.", "It means the prior pull was set too weakly, letting the knobs move rather further than the data actually supports."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With four knobs and four coupled targets there is a surface of achievable error combinations, and improving one point on it moves others.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'The tuned residual is not zero. What is the remaining amount made of?', '["The frontier, where the parameterisation runs out, rather than solver error.", "Measurement noise in the four targets, which no model can or should fit.", "The prior pull''s own contribution, which holds the knobs some way off the true minimum.", "Numerical error from the saturation pressure''s bisection tolerance."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Running the solver longer, or from a different start, lands in the same place. Restarting from a different point is how you tell a solver that stopped early from a model at its frontier.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'Which five lines does a tuned model''s write-up need?', '["The knobs, the bounds, the solver, the iteration count and the final residual.", "The composition, the characterization, the conditions, the train and the targets.", "The model, the fluid, the date, the engine version and who ran it.", "What was tuned, against what, the ledger, what was held fixed, and what it cost."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Without the scope a reader assumes the whole model was fitted. Without the targets they cannot tell what it was fitted to. Without the cost they will find it themselves.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'A tuned model ships with no write-up. What goes wrong later?', '["The parameters cannot be reproduced, so the model cannot be rebuilt if the file is lost.", "The tuning may be applied twice, compounding the adjustment without anybody noticing.", "A tuned critical temperature is a fitted parameter wearing the name of a measured quantity, and a later reader treats it as characterization.", "The engine''s gates fail on the next release, since the tuned values are not in the fixture."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That is why the report goes with the model as well as in the study document. Otherwise it becomes an untraceable set of parameters within a year.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'One of the six provenance tiers is never a graded capstone field. Which, and why?', '["Published method, since a method with no independent check cannot support a graded field.", "Screening: an estimate nobody has checked against anything.", "Lab tuned, since a tuned value depends on the measurements it was tuned to.", "Oracle gated, since agreement between implementations says nothing about the physics."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The other three are graded somewhere in the course. Oracle gated agreement genuinely says nothing about physics, and that is a limitation of the tier rather than a reason to refuse it.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'A module teaches a quantity thoroughly and grades none of it. What is the reasoning?', '["Because grading says producing it correctly is a competence worth certifying, which implies the number is worth producing.", "Because grading needs a tolerance and a screening quantity cannot support one.", "Because a graded field must be reproducible to sixteen figures and screening values vary.", "Because the grading chassis cannot express a tolerance anywhere near wide enough to accommodate a factor-of-two uncertainty."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A screening quantity is perfectly reproducible and a wide tolerance is expressible. What is false is the implication that the number is worth producing to a standard.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'Trace the LBC calculation from the C7+ characterization. Where does the uncertainty blow up?', '["Because it was fitted to gases and is extrapolated to liquids without justification.", "Because it depends on densities the volume translation has already adjusted.", "Because no two published implementations of it agree, so the arithmetic itself is genuinely in question.", "Because the reduced density needs a correlated critical volume and then enters a fourth power."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Order ten percent on gas, up to a factor of two on oil. The error amplification is severe and worst for liquids, where the reduced density is highest.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'What is gated about LBC and what is not?', '["The answer is gated against the study''s viscosities and the implementation only reviewed.", "Both are gated, the answer against the eight Coats and Smart fluids in the harness.", "The arithmetic is verified by transcription and NIST anchors; the answer is not, because no measured viscosities exist for this fluid.", "Neither is gated, which is what the screening label means."]'::jsonb, 2, 'An implementation can be provably correct and still produce a number nobody should book. That distinction is the whole tier system in one example.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'You need an oil viscosity for an ordinary black oil. Correlation chain or compositional model?', '["The Beal, Beggs-Robinson and Vasquez-Beggs chain, which is published method rather than screening.", "The LBC calculation, since it uses the composition and carries more information.", "Either, since both are correlations and the tier difference reflects documentation.", "Neither of them, since a black oil viscosity should always be measured in a laboratory rather than predicted."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The compositional model''s advantage lies elsewhere. Rigour in one part of a model does not transfer to another part in the same file.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'Why does the black-oil separator get the total right and the split wrong?', '["Because the total is measured and the split is predicted, so only the split can be wrong.", "Because it telescopes to the bubble point solution gas ratio by construction, while the split is a rule rather than a flash.", "Because the total is a sum of stage volumes whose individual errors cancel exactly.", "Because computing the split needs a full composition and computing the total does not, so only the split is ever unavailable."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A black-oil description has two pseudo-components rather than eleven, so there is nothing to flash. The compositional separator train is the rigorous counterpart and its cost is a composition.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'A screening estimate is going into a study document. In what form?', '["To full precision with a footnote, so the calculation can be reproduced exactly.", "As a range across plausible inputs, since one value implies a determinacy it lacks.", "Rounded to one or two figures, with the method named and the status said in words.", "In an appendix only, so it cannot be lifted into a summary and quoted as measured."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Quoting a screening number to sixteen digits is a claim about it whether or not one is intended. Inconsistent precision within a table is a feature when the confidence is inconsistent.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'Capstone field 1 asks for the tuned saturation pressure. What is the check?', '["It is close to the measurement but not equal, and slightly BELOW it rather than above.", "It equals the measured 2634.65 psia exactly, since the psat target dominates the whole objective.", "It is above the measured value, since the untuned bias is only partly corrected.", "It falls between the untuned value and the measurement, nearer the untuned one."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tuned error on the saturation pressure is negative. Reporting the untuned 2791.10 or the measured 2634.65 are the two likely mistakes.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'A learner submits 793.8 scf/stb for the tuned gas-oil ratio. How do you know it is wrong?', '["It sits above 768, since tuning raises gas liberation to match the saturation pressure.", "It equals the separator gas plus the tank gas, which every train satisfies by identity.", "It falls between the four separator tests in the report, which bracket the tuned value.", "It sits BELOW the measured 768 scf/stb, where the untuned value sat above it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'If the answer is above 768 you have the untuned one. Omitting the stock tank stage is the other mistake and it pushes the answer below the truth for a different reason.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'The regression starts the volume shift at 0.15389683656773767. Where should it finish?', '["It is larger than the characterized value, since the correction increases the translation.", "Smaller than it started, because the untuned oil was too dense and the correction lightens it.", "It equals the characterized value exactly, since the prior pull holds an under-determined knob still.", "It is negative, since a positive shift would make the liquid denser rather than lighter."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Reporting the untuned characterized shift of 0.15389683656773767 is the likely mistake, because that is where the regression started rather than where it finished.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'How do you know field 5 is not one of the multipliers?', '["Because the multipliers are dimensionless and this carries units of reciprocal temperature.", "Because the multipliers are reported to three figures and this one to six.", "Because the multipliers are near one and this is a few hundredths, the ordinary scale for a hydrocarbon interaction parameter.", "Because the multipliers are bounded above one and this knob is bounded below it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'All four are dimensionless and the multiplier bounds straddle one. Scale is the distinguishing feature.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'Take the tier''s six graded fields together. What is true of every one of them?', '["Each asks for a value AFTER the regression, and each likely mistake is the value before it or the target it aimed at.", "Each depends on the volume shift, the knob that moves furthest and dominates the fit.", "Each can be read from the tuning explorer''s ledger without computation outside it.", "Each one has its own counterpart at the Professional tier, and the pair of them is the comparison actually being tested."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Three numbers exist for most of these quantities and only one is the answer. Write down which of untuned, measured or tuned you have before submitting.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'A draft describes the tuned model as validated against the study. What is wrong with that?', '["Calibrated, since the tuning was against measurements rather than another model''s output.", "Fitted, since it suggests a statistical procedure where this was a bounded optimisation.", "Matched, since the residual is not zero and the word implies exact reproduction.", "Validated."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The model reproduces its four targets by construction, which is a calibration and cannot fail. A validation needs something the model was not tuned to.'),
  ('fluid', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'Across all three tiers of this course, what never happened?', '["Each tuned the model further, so the description improved monotonically across the three.", "Nobody measured anything in any of them; what changed is how much was known about where each number came from.", "Each worked on a different fluid, testing the correlations across a range of oil types.", "Each graded a different class of quantity, moving from volumes through transport properties to full compositions."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Associate tier ran correlations on a designed fluid, the Professional tier measured an untuned model against a real study, and the Expert tier opened the model and tuned it.');
